# vibrational_modes — chain-verified vibrational normal-mode frequencies (cm⁻¹)
# (Spike #39 POC: Class L dynamical-matrix eigendecomposition against NIST CCCBDB).
#
# Each row stores: molecular substrate (InChIKey + formula + geometry + atomic_symbols
# + bond_length_a + bond_angle_deg + GVFF force constants) + predicted_frequencies_cm-1
# + measured_frequencies_cm-1 (NIST CCCBDB). The chain `normal_mode_frequencies_xy2`
# (see descriptor.toml [catalog]) computes the GF mass-weighted Hessian eigvals via
# Wilson-Decius-Cross §6 closed-form analytic secular equation; CI re-runs the chain
# and bit-exact-compares the result to predicted_frequencies_cm-1.
#
# The measured-vs-predicted residual is the falsification gauge — within ~5%
# (stretches) / ~10% (bends) is the expected GVFF-only accuracy floor (no Urey-Bradley
# / cross-coupling refinement). The POC demonstrates structural validity of the
# catalog shape, not perfect quantitative match.
#
# Per `[[feedback_every_doc_edit_faces_falsification]]`: chain spec lives in
# descriptor.toml; expected outputs bit-exactly verifiable from atomic-mass +
# force-constant inputs.
#
# Per `[[user_stance_information_instrument_form_function_bound]]`: molecular
# substrate is the binding-level partition; underlying Class L (channel-eigenbasis
# primitive) is the substrate-portable identity reused with the dynamical-matrix
# operator (vs. graph-Laplacian operator from Spike #38).
#
# Per `[[feedback_no_privileged_primitive_classes]]`: this is NOT a 15th class —
# the vocabulary stays at 14 (A-N). Molecular-modeling catalogs are a new partition
# at the substrate-binding layer.
#
# Citation provenance (per `[[feedback_pdf_extraction_citation_discipline]]`):
# - Wilson-Decius-Cross §6 (closed-form XY2 secular equation): textbook, not
#   autonomously PDF-extracted — historical canonical reference flagged honestly.
# - Herzberg II 1945 (force constants): textbook, not autonomously PDF-extracted.
# - Shimanouchi NSRDS-NBS 39 (DOI 10.6028/NBS.NSRDS.39): NIST NSRDS canonical
#   tabulation; cited by NIST CCCBDB as the source for H2O experimental ν1+ν2.
# - Huber-Herzberg 1979: textbook; cited by NIST CCCBDB as the source for H2O ν3.
# - NIST CCCBDB (cccbdb.nist.gov): US-government open data per
#   `[[reference_autonomous_validation_tos_landscape]]`; verified via WebFetch
#   for H2O (casno=7732185) and CO2 (casno=124389) on 2026-05-17.

